description.narrative:
The State Government of Telengana adopted an integrated approach to FPRW through state labour department orders to District Magistrates (DM) in 2019, and  rolled it out as a pilot in 2020 in Adilabad, Warangal Rural, Nalgonda and Gadwal districts. In 2020-21, DMs  and the state labour department implemented the integrated approach through capacity building of district officials on FPRW. This led to  changes in Capacity Building: 
a) Training to district officials on FPRW conducted;
b) changes made in awareness-raising on the benefits of capacity building on FPRW in an integrated manner instead of only on one pillar;
c) the scope of the state resource centre on child labour expanded to bonded labour and to jointly address both  issues; 
d) The Government raised awareness on the integrated approach through media briefings, public announcements; 
e) Central Trade Unions trained and mobilised  small farmers and agriculture sector workers, majority of informal work to form association and participate in dialogue for negotiation. Thousands of workers have formed Trade Union and participated in wage negotiation in 2020-21.  Unions have formed a joint forum to promote the integrated strategy of FPRW by organizing workers and farmers in the agriculture sector.Farmers have been capacitated to join existing and/or form new Associations like FPOs to enable them to adopt FPRW and negotiate with supply chain actors collectively for fair price of cotton and essential inputs. 25-30  FPOs, over three thousand farmers, integrate FPRW into sustainability dimensions of cotton farming.
f) Capacity building-cum-awareness programmes brought clarity and confidence to cotton-growing workers on non-discrimination and gender based wage discrimination, prepared women workers to engage in dialogue and negotiation.Officials of rural governance at village level are sensitised to promote and monitor non-discriminatory practices in coordination with farmers and workers in cotton growing areas With the support of a public-private partnership, the ILO contributed through technical support to the work in Telengana. Key contributions included:
- Advocating with Government, unions and other key partners for improving their understanding and creating an enabling environment for adopting the four pillars of FPRW in an integrated manner;
- More than three hundred (300) workshops/consultations organized with Government, Trade Unions, CSOs, Research organizations at state, district, Mandal and village level to generate awareness and build capacity for implementation of all the four pillars of FPRW in integrated manner.
- Special campaigns on elimination of child labour were organized jointly by ILO and Govt. of Telangana through electronic & print media, social media, involving key policy makers and film and television actors and singers, with an outreach of more than 900,000 people.
